ct whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSRegression(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that 3 PLS packages\n    # provided in the R language (R-project):\n    #     - \"mixOmics\" with function pls(X, Y, mode = \"regression\")\n    #     - \"plspm \" with function plsreg2(X, Y)\n    #     - \"pls\" with function oscorespls.fit(X, Y)\n\n    def __init__(\n        self, n_components=2, *, scale=True, max_iter=500, tol=1e-06, copy=True\n    ):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, Y):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound class `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass PLSCanonical(_PLS):\n\n    # This implementation provides the same results that the \"plspm\" package\n    # provided in the R language (R-project), using the function plsca(X, Y).\n    # Results are equal or collinear with the function\n    # ``pls(..., mode = \"canonical\")`` of the \"mixOmics\" package. The\n    # difference relies in the fact that mixOmics implementation does not\n    # exactly implement the Wold algorithm since it does not normalize\n    # y_weights to one.\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        n_components=2,\n        *,\n        scale=True,\n        algorithm=\"nipals\",\n        max_iter=500,\n        tol=1e-06,\n        copy=True,\n    ):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_fit_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3517 | def check_fit_non_negative(name, estimator_orig):\n3518 |     # Check that proper warning is raised for non-negative X\n3519 |     # when tag requires_positive_X is present\n3520 |     X = np.array([[-1.0, 1], [-1.0, 1]])\n3521 |     y = np.array([1, 2])\n3522 |     estimator = clone(estimator_orig)\n3523 |     with raises(ValueError):\n3524 |         estimator.fit(X, y)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_non_negative`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1336 | def check_non_negative(X, whom):\n1337 |     \"\"\"\n1338 |     Check if there is any negative value in an array.\n1339 | \n1340 |     Parameters\n1341 |     ----------\n1342 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n1343 |         Input data.\n1344 | \n1345 |     whom : str\n1346 |         Who passed X to this function.\n1347 |     \"\"\"\n1348 |     # avoid X.min() on sparse matrix since it also sorts the indices\n1349 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n1350 |         if X.format in [\"lil\", \"dok\"]:\n1351 |             X = X.tocsr()\n1352 |         if X.data.size == 0:\n1353 |             X_min = 0\n1354 |         else:\n1355 |             X_min = X.data.min()\n1356 |     else:\n1357 |         X_min = X.min()\n1358 | \n1359 |     if X_min < 0:\n1360 |         raise ValueError(\"Negative values in data passed to %s\" % whom)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1918 | def _generate_get_feature_names_out(estimator, n_features_out, input_features=None):\n1919 |     \"\"\"Generate feature names out for estimator using the estimator name as the prefix.\n1920 | \n1921 |     The input_feature names are validated but not used. This function is useful\n1922 |     for estimators that generate their own names based on `n_features_out`, i.e. PCA.\n1923 | \n1924 |     Parameters\n1925 |     ----------\n1926 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1927 |         Estimator producing output feature names.\n1928 | \n1929 |     n_feature_out : int\n1930 |         Number of feature names out.\n1931 | \n1932 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n1933 |         Only used to validate feature names with `estimator.feature_names_in_`.\n1934 | \n1935 |     Returns\n1936 |     -------\n1937 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n1938 |         Feature names in.\n1939 |     \"\"\"\n1940 |     _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features, generate_names=False)\n1941 |     estimator_name = estimator.__class__.__name__.lower()\n1942 |     return np.asarray(\n1943 |         [f\"{estimator_name}{i}\" for i in range(n_features_out)], dtype=object\n1944 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n634 | def fit(self, X, Y):\n635 |         \"\"\"Fit model to data.\n636 | \n637 |         Parameters\n638 |         ----------\n639 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n640 |             Training vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n641 |             `n_features` is the number of predictors.\n642 | \n643 |         Y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_targets)\n644 |             Target vectors, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n645 |             `n_targets` is the number of response variables.\n646 | \n647 |         Returns\n648 |         -------\n649 |         self : object\n650 |             Fitted model.\n651 |         \"\"\"\n652 |         super().fit(X, Y)\n653 |         # expose the fitted attributes `x_scores_` and `y_scores_`\n654 |         self.x_scores_ = self._x_scores\n655 |         self.y_scores_ = self._y_scores\n656 |         return self\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me explore the dependency structure around NMF to understand the implementation flow better.\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF']</parameter>\n<parameter=direction>both</parameter>\n<parameter=dependency_type_filter>['imports', 'invokes', 'inherits']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "explore_tree_structure", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 inherits \u2500\u2500 sklearn/base.py:TransformerMixin\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 inherits \u2500\u2500 sklearn/base.py:_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 inherits \u2500\u2500 sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/datasets.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/decomposition.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 sklearn/manifold/_t_sne.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 s