orts-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_tsne_mnist.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_plot_incremental_pca.py\n\u2502                 \u251c\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_kernel_pca_solvers_time_vs_n_components.py\n\u2502                 \u2514\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_kernel_pca_solvers_time_vs_n_samples.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 invokes-by \u2500\u2500 sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization\n\u2502                 \u2514\u2500\u2500 imports-by \u2500\u2500 sklearn/decomposition/__init__.py\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 inherits-by \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py:_PGNMF\n"}, {"content": "Let me get the full content of the NMF class to understand its implementation better.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`.\n```\n1120 | class NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n1121 |     \"\"\"Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n1122 | \n1123 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n1124 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n1125 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n1126 | \n1127 |     The objective function is:\n1128 | \n1129 |         .. math::\n1130 | \n1131 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n1132 | \n1133 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n1134 | \n1135 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n1136 | \n1137 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n1138 | \n1139 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n1140 | \n1141 |     Where:\n1142 | \n1143 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n1144 | \n1145 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n1146 | \n1147 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}` may represent\n1148 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n1149 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n1150 | \n1151 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n1152 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n1153 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n1154 | \n1155 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n1156 |     and H.\n1157 | \n1158 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n1159 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n1160 |     matrix X is transposed.\n1161 | \n1162 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <NMF>`.\n1163 | \n1164 |     Parameters\n1165 |     ----------\n1166 |     n_components : int, default=None\n1167 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n1168 |         are kept.\n1169 | \n1170 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n1171 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n1172 |         Default: None.\n1173 |         Valid options:\n1174 | \n1175 |         - `None`: 'nndsvda' if n_components <= min(n_samples, n_features),\n1176 |           otherwise random.\n1177 | \n1178 |         - `'random'`: non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n1179 |           sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n1180 | \n1181 |         - `'nndsvd'`: Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n1182 |           initialization (better for sparseness)\n1183 | \n1184 |         - `'nndsvda'`: NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n1185 |           (better when sparsity is not desired)\n1186 | \n1187 |         - `'nndsvdar'` NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n1188 |           (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n1189 |           for when sparsity is not desired)\n1190 | \n1191 |         - `'custom'`: use custom matrices W and H\n1192 | \n1193 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n1194 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n1195 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n1196 | \n1197 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n1198 |         Numerical solver to use:\n1199 |         'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver.\n1200 |         'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n1201 | \n1202 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1203 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n1204 | \n1205 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1206 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n1207 | \n1208 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1209 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1210 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1211 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1212 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1213 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1214 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1215 | \n1216 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1217 | \n1218 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1219 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1220 | \n1221 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1222 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1223 | \n1224 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1225 |         Used for initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1226 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1227 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1228 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1229 | \n1230 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1231 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to\n1232 |         have no regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`,\n1233 |         the regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1234 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1235 | \n1236 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1237 |            *alpha* used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1238 | \n1239 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1240 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1241 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1242 | \n1243 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1244 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1245 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1246 | \n1247 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1248 | \n1249 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1250 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1251 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1252 |         `alpha_W`.\n1253 | \n1254 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1255 | \n1256 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1257 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1258 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1259 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1260 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1261 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1262 | \n1263 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1264 |            Regularization parameter *l1_ratio* used in the Coordinate Descent\n1265 |            solver.\n1266 | \n1267 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1268 |         Whether to be verbose.\n1269 | \n1270 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1271 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1272 | \n1273 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n1274 |            *shuffle* parameter used in the Coordinate Descent solver.\n1275 | \n1276 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation', None}, \\\n1277 |                      default='both'\n1278 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1279 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1280 | \n1281 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1282 | \n1283 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1284 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1285 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1286 | \n1287 |     Attributes\n1288 |     ----------\n1289 |     components_ : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1290 |         Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n1291 | \n1292 |     n_components_ : int\n1293 |         The number of components. It is same as the `n_components` parameter\n1294 |         if it was given. Otherwise, it will be same as the number of\n1295 |         features.\n1296 | \n1297 |     reconstruction_err_ : float\n1298 |         Frobenius norm of the matrix difference, or beta-divergence, between\n1299 |         the training data ``X`` and the reconstructed data ``WH`` from\n1300 |         the fitted model.\n1301 | \n1302 |     n_iter_ : int\n1303 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1304 | \n1305 |     n_features_in_ : int\n1306 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`.\n1307 | \n1308 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1309 | \n1310 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n1311 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Defined only when `X`\n1312 |         has feature names that are all strings.\n1313 | \n1314 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1315 | \n1316 |     See Also\n1317 |     --------\n1318 |     DictionaryLearning : Find a dictionary that sparsely encodes data.\n1319 |     MiniBatchSparsePCA : Mini-batch Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1320 |     PCA : Principal component analysis.\n1321 |     SparseCoder : Find a sparse representation of data from a fixed,\n1322 |         precomputed dictionary.\n1323 |     SparsePCA : Sparse Principal Components Analysis.\n1324 |     TruncatedSVD : Dimensionality reduction using truncated SVD.\n1325 | \n1326 |     References\n1327 |     ----------\n1328 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1329 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1330 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1331 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1332 | \n1333 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1334 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1335 | \n1336 |     Examples\n1337 |     --------\n1338 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1339 |     >>> X = np.array([[1, 1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1340 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import NMF\n1341 |     >>> model = NMF(n_components=2, init='random', random_state=0)\n1342 |     >>> W = model.fit_transform(X)\n1343 |     >>> H = model.components_\n1344 |     \"\"\"\n1345 | \n1346 |     def __init__(\n1347 |         self,\n1348 |         n_components=None,\n1349 |         *,\n1350 |         init=None,\n1351 |         solver=\"cd\",\n1352 |         beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n1353 |         tol=1e-4,\n1354 |         max_iter=200,\n1355 |         random_state=None,\n1356 |         alpha=\"deprecated\",\n1357 |         alpha_W=0.0,\n1358 |         alpha_H=\"same\",\n1359 |         l1_ratio=0.0,\n1360 |         verbose=0,\n1361 |         shuffle=False,\n1362 |         regularization=\"deprecated\",\n1363 |     ):\n1364 |         self.n_components = n_components\n1365 |         self.init = init\n1366 |         self.solver = solver\n1367 |         self.beta_loss = beta_loss\n1368 |         self.tol = tol\n1369 |         self.max_iter = max_iter\n1370 |         self.random_state = random_state\n1371 |         self.alpha = alpha\n1372 |         self.alpha_W = alpha_W\n1373 |         self.alpha_H = alpha_H\n1374 |         self.l1_ratio = l1_ratio\n1375 |         self.verbose = verbose\n1376 |         self.shuffle = shuffle\n1377 |         self.regularization = regularization\n1378 | \n1379 |     def _more_tags(self):\n1380 |         return {\"requires_positive_X\": True}\n1381 | \n1382 |     def _check_params(self, X):\n1383 |         # n_components\n1384 |         self._n_components = self.n_components\n1385 |         if self._n_components is None:\n1386 |             self._n_components = X.shape[1]\n1387 |         if (\n1388 |             not isinstance(self._n_components, numbers.Integral)\n1389 |             or self._n_components <= 0\n1390 |         ):\n1391 |             raise ValueError(\n1392 |                 \"Number of components must be a positive integer; got \"\n1393 |                 f\"(n_components={self._n_components!r})\"\n1394 |             )\n1395 | \n1396 |         # max_iter\n1397 |         if not isinstance(self.max_iter, numbers.Integral) or self.max_iter < 0:\n1398 |             raise ValueError(\n1399 |                 \"Maximum number of iterations must be a positive \"\n1400 |                 f\"integer; got (max_iter={self.max_iter!r})\"\n1401 |             )\n1402 | \n1403 |         # tol\n1404 |         if not isinstance(self.tol, numbers.Number) or self.tol < 0:\n1405 |             raise ValueError(\n1406 |                 \"Tolerance for stopping criteria must be positive; got \"\n1407 |                 f\"(tol={self.tol!r})\"\n1408 |             )\n1409 | \n1410 |         # beta_loss\n1411 |         self._beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(self.beta_loss)\n1412 | \n1413 |         # solver\n1414 |         allowed_solver = (\"cd\", \"mu\")\n1415 |         if self.solver not in allowed_solver:\n1416 |             raise ValueError(\n1417 |                 f\"Invalid solver parameter: got {self.solver!r} instead of one of \"\n1418 |                 f\"{allowed_solver}\"\n1419 |             )\n1420 |         if self.solver != \"mu\" and self.beta_loss not in (2, \"frobenius\"):\n1421 |             # 'mu' is the only solver that handles other beta losses than 'frobenius'\n1422 |             raise ValueError(\n1423 |                 f\"Invalid beta_loss parameter: solver {self.solver!r} does not handle \"\n1424 |                 f\"beta_loss = {self.beta_loss!r}\"\n1425 |             )\n1426 |         if self.solver == \"mu\" and self.init == \"nndsvd\":\n1427 |             warnings.warn(\n1428 |                 \"The multiplicative update ('mu') solver cannot update \"\n1429 |                 \"zeros present in the initialization, and so leads to \"\n1430 |                 \"poorer results when used jointly with init='nndsvd'. \"\n1431 |                 \"You may try init='nndsvda' or init='nndsvdar' instead.\",\n1432 |                 UserWarning,\n1433 |             )\n1434 | \n1435 |         # alpha and regularization are deprecated in favor of alpha_W and alpha_H\n1436 |         # TODO clean up in 1.2\n1437 |         if self.alpha != \"deprecated\":\n1438 |             warnings.warn(\n1439 |                 \"`alpha` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be removed \"\n1440 |                 \"in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1441 |                 FutureWarning,\n1442 |             )\n1443 |             alpha = self.alpha\n1444 |         else:\n1445 |             alpha = 0.0\n1446 | \n1447 |         if self.regularization != \"deprecated\":\n1448 |             warnings.warn(\n1449 |                 \"`regularization` was deprecated in version 1.0 and will be \"\n1450 |                 \"removed in 1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead\",\n1451 |                 FutureWarning,\n1452 |             )\n1453 |             allowed_regularization = (\"both\", \"components\", \"transformation\", None)\n1454 |             if self.regularization not in allowed_regularization:\n1455 |                 raise ValueError(\n1456 |                     f\"Invalid regularization parameter: got {self.regularization!r} \"\n1457 |                     f\"instead of one of {allowed_regularization}\"\n1458 |                 )\n1459 |             regularization = self.regularization\n1460 |         else:\n1461 |             regularization = \"both\"\n1462 | \n1463 |         (\n1464 |             self._l1_reg_W,\n1465 |             self._l1_reg_H,\n1466 |             self._l2_reg_W,\n1467 |             self._l2_reg_H,\n1468 |         ) = _compute_regularization(\n1469 |             alpha, self.alpha_W, self.alpha_H, self.l1_ratio, regularization\n1470 |         )\n1471 | \n1472 |         return self\n1473 | \n1474 |     def _check_w_h(self, X, W, H, update_H):\n1475 |         # check W and H, or initialize them\n1476 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1477 |         if self.init == \"custom\" and update_H:\n1478 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1479 |             _check_init(W, (n_samples, self._n_components), \"NMF (input W)\")\n1480 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype or W.dtype != X.dtype:\n1481 |                 raise TypeError(\n1482 |                     \"H and W should have the same dtype as X. Got \"\n1483 |                     \"H.dtype = {} and W.dtype = {}.\".format(H.dtype, W.dtype)\n1484 |                 )\n1485 |         elif not update_H:\n1486 |             _check_init(H, (self._n_components, n_features), \"NMF (input H)\")\n1487 |             if H.dtype != X.dtype:\n1488 |                 raise TypeError(\n1489 |                     \"H should have the same dtype as X. Got H.dtype = {}.\".format(\n1490 |                         H.dtype\n1491 |                     )\n1492 |                 )\n1493 |             # 'mu' solver should not be initialized by zeros\n1494 |             if self.solver == \"mu\":\n1495 |                 avg = np.sqrt(X.mean() / self._n_components)\n1496 |                 W = np.full((n_samples, self._n_components), avg, dtype=X.dtype)\n1497 |             else:\n1498 |                 W = np.zeros((n_samples, self._n_components), dtype=X.dtype)\n1499 |         else:\n1500 |             W, H = _initialize_nmf(\n1501 |                 X, self._n_components, init=self.init, random_state=self.random_state\n1502 |             )\n1503 |         return W, H\n1504 | \n1505 |     def _scale_regularization(self, X):\n1506 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n1507 |         if self.alpha_W != 0 or self.alpha_H != \"same\":\n1508 |             # if alpha_W or alpha_H is not left to its default value we ignore alpha\n1509 |             # and regularization, and we scale the regularization terms.\n1510 |             l1_reg_W = n_features * self._l1_reg_W\n1511 |             l1_reg_H = n_samples * self._l1_reg_H\n1512 |             l2_reg_W = n_features * self._l2_reg_W\n1513 |             l2_reg_H = n_samples * self._l2_reg_H\n1514 |         else:\n1515 |             # Otherwise we keep the old behavior with no scaling\n1516 |             # TODO remove in 1.2\n1517 |             l1_reg_W = self._l1_reg_W\n1518 |             l1_reg_H = self._l1_reg_H\n1519 |             l2_reg_W = self._l2_reg_W\n1520 |             l2_reg_H = self._l2_reg_H\n1521 | \n1522 |         return l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H\n1523 | \n1524 |     def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None):\n1525 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1526 | \n1527 |         This is more efficient than calling fit followed by transform.\n1528 | \n1529 |         Parameters\n1530 |         ----------\n1531 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1532 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1533 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1534 | \n1535 |         y : Ignored\n1536 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n1537 | \n1538 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1539 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1540 | \n1541 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1542 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1543 | \n1544 |         Returns\n1545 |         -------\n1546 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1547 |             Transformed data.\n1548 |         \"\"\"\n1549 |         X = self._validate_data(\n1550 |             X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32]\n1551 |         )\n1552 | \n1553 |         with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1554 |             W, H, n_iter = self._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H)\n1555 | \n1556 |         self.reconstruction_err_ = _beta_divergence(\n1557 |             X, W, H, self._beta_loss, square_root=True\n1558 |         )\n1559 | \n1560 |         self.n_components_ = H.shape[0]\n1561 |         self.components_ = H\n1562 |         self.n_iter_ = n_iter\n1563 | \n1564 |         return W\n1565 | \n1566 |     def _fit_transform(self, X, y=None, W=None, H=None, update_H=True):\n1567 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X and returns the transformed data.\n1568 | \n1569 |         Parameters\n1570 |         ----------\n1571 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1572 |             Data matrix to be decomposed\n1573 | \n1574 |         y : Ignored\n1575 | \n1576 |         W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1577 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1578 | \n1579 |         H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1580 |             If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n1581 |             If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n1582 | \n1583 |         update_H : bool, default=True\n1584 |             If True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses,\n1585 |             this corresponds to a call to the 'fit_transform' method.\n1586 |             If False, only W will be estimated, this corresponds to a call\n1587 |             to the 'transform' method.\n1588 | \n1589 |         Returns\n1590 |         -------\n1591 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1592 |             Transformed data.\n1593 | \n1594 |         H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1595 |             Factorization matrix, sometimes called 'dictionary'.\n1596 | \n1597 |         n_iter_ : int\n1598 |             Actual number of iterations.\n1599 |         \"\"\"\n1600 |         check_non_negative(X, \"NMF (input X)\")\n1601 | \n1602 |         # check parameters\n1603 |         self._check_params(X)\n1604 | \n1605 |         if X.min() == 0 and self._beta_loss <= 0:\n1606 |             raise ValueError(\n1607 |                 \"When beta_loss <= 0 and X contains zeros, \"\n1608 |                 \"the solver may diverge. Please add small values \"\n1609 |                 \"to X, or use a positive beta_loss.\"\n1610 |             )\n1611 | \n1612 |         # initialize or check W and H\n1613 |         W, H = self._check_w_h(X, W, H, update_H)\n1614 | \n1615 |         # scale the regularization terms\n1616 |         l1_reg_W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_W, l2_reg_H = self._scale_regularization(X)\n1617 | \n1618 |         if self.solver == \"cd\":\n1619 |             W, H, n_iter = _fit_coordinate_descent(\n1620 |                 X,\n1621 |                 W,\n1622 |                 H,\n1623 |                 self.tol,\n1624 |                 self.max_iter,\n1625 |                 l1_reg_W,\n1626 |                 l1_reg_H,\n1627 |                 l2_reg_W,\n1628 |                 l2_reg_H,\n1629 |                 update_H=update_H,\n1630 |                 verbose=self.verbose,\n1631 |                 shuffle=self.shuffle,\n1632 |                 random_state=self.random_state,\n1633 |             )\n1634 |         elif self.solver == \"mu\":\n1635 |             W, H, n_iter = _fit_multiplicative_update(\n1636 |                 X,\n1637 |                 W,\n1638 |                 H,\n1639 |                 self._beta_loss,\n1640 |                 self.max_iter,\n1641 |                 self.tol,\n1642 |                 l1_reg_W,\n1643 |                 l1_reg_H,\n1644 |                 l2_reg_W,\n1645 |                 l2_reg_H,\n1646 |                 update_H=update_H,\n1647 |                 verbose=self.verbose,\n1648 |             )\n1649 |         else:\n1650 |             raise ValueError(\"Invalid solver parameter '%s'.\" % self.solver)\n1651 | \n1652 |         if n_iter == self.max_iter and self.tol > 0:\n1653 |             warnings.warn(\n1654 |                 \"Maximum number of iterations %d reached. Increase \"\n1655 |                 \"it to improve convergence.\"\n1656 |                 % self.max_iter,\n1657 |                 ConvergenceWarning,\n1658 |             )\n1659 | \n1660 |         return W, H, n_iter\n1661 | \n1662 |     def fit(self, X, y=None, **params):\n1663 |         \"\"\"Learn a NMF model for the data X.\n1664 | \n1665 |         Parameters\n1666 |         ----------\n1667 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1668 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1669 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1670 | \n1671 |         y : Ignored\n1672 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n1673 | \n1674 |         **params : kwargs\n1675 |             Parameters (keyword arguments) and values passed to\n1676 |             the fit_transform instance.\n1677 | \n1678 |         Returns\n1679 |         -------\n1680 |         self : object\n1681 |             Returns the instance itself.\n1682 |         \"\"\"\n1683 |         self.fit_transform(X, **params)\n1684 |         return self\n1685 | \n1686 |     def transform(self, X):\n1687 |         \"\"\"Transform the data X according to the fitted NMF model.\n1688 | \n1689 |         Parameters\n1690 |         ----------\n1691 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1692 |             Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples\n1693 |             and `n_features` is the number of features.\n1694 | \n1695 |         Returns\n1696 |         -------\n1697 |         W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1698 |             Transformed data.\n1699 |         \"\"\"\n1700 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n1701 |         X = self._validate_data(\n1702 |             X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32], reset=False\n1703 |         )\n1704 | \n1705 |         with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1706 |             W, *_ = self._fit_transform(X, H=self.components_, update_H=False)\n1707 | \n1708 |         return W\n1709 | \n1710 |     def inverse_transform(self, W):\n1711 |         \"\"\"Transform data back to its original space.\n1712 | \n1713 |         .. versionadded:: 0.18\n1714 | \n1715 |         Parameters\n1716 |         ----------\n1717 |         W : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1718 |             Transformed data matrix.\n1719 | \n1720 |         Returns\n1721 |         -------\n1722 |         X : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1723 |             Returns a data matrix of the original shape.\n1724 |         \"\"\"\n1725 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n1726 |         return np.dot(W, self.components_)\n1727 | \n1728 |     @property\n1729 |     def _n_features_out(self):\n1730 |         \"\"\"Number of transformed output features.\"\"\"\n1731 |         return self.components_.shape[0]\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check if there are any online-specific implementations or related code:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['online', 'MiniBatchNMF']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._em_step\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation\nbenchmarks/bench_online_ocsvm.py:impl:190\nsklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:_update_dict\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._check_params\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation.fit\nsklearn/naive_bayes.py:GaussianNB\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/datasets/_arff_parser.py:_sparse_data_to_array\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._em_step\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation\nbenchmarks/bench_online_ocsvm.py:impl:190\nsklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:_update_dict\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._check_params\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation.fit\nsklearn/naive_bayes.py:GaussianNB\n##Searching for term \"online\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/cluster/plot_dict_face_patches.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | Online learning of a dictionary of parts of faces\n 3 | =================================================\n 4 | \n 5 | This example uses a large dataset of faces to learn a set of 20 x 20\n 6 | images patches that constitute faces.\n 7 | \n 8 | From the programming standpoint, it is interesting because it shows how\n 9 | to use the online API of the scikit-learn to process a very large\n10 | dataset by chunks. The way we proceed is that we load an image at a time\n11 | and extract randomly 50 patches from this image. Once we have accumulated\n12 | 500 of these patches (using 10 images), we run the\n13 | :func:`~sklearn.cluster.MiniBatchKMeans.partial_fit` method\n14 | of the online KMeans object, MiniBatchKMeans.\n15 | \n16 | The verbose setting on the MiniBatchKMeans enables us to see that some\n17 | clusters are reassigned during the successive calls to\n18 | partial-fit. This is because the number of patches that they represent\n19 | has become too low, and it is better to choose a random new\n20 | cluster.\n21 | \n22 | \"\"\"\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | # Load the data\n26 | # -------------\n27 | \n28 | from sklearn import datasets\n29 | \n30 | faces = datasets.fetch_olivetti_faces()\n31 | \n32 | # %%\n33 | # Learn the dictionary of images\n34 | # ------------------------------\n35 | \n36 | import time\n37 | \n38 | import numpy as np\n39 | \n40 | from sklearn.cluster import MiniBatchKMeans\n41 | from sklearn.feature_extraction.image import extract_patches_2d\n42 | \n43 | print(\"Learning the dictionary... \")\n44 | rng = np.random.RandomState(0)\n45 | kmeans = MiniBatchKMeans(n_clusters=81, random_state=rng, verbose=True)\n46 | patch_size = (20, 20)\n47 | \n48 | buffer = []\n49 | t0 = time.time()\n50 | \n51 | # The online learning part: cycle over the whole dataset 6 times\n52 | index = 0\n53 | for _ in range(6):\n54 |     for img in faces.images:\n55 |         data = extract_patches_2d(img, patch_size, max_patches=50, random_state=rng)\n56 |         data = np.reshape(data, (len(data), -1))\n57 |         buffer.append(data)\n58 |         index += 1\n59 |         if index % 10 == 0:\n60 |             data = np.concatenate(buffer, axis=0)\n61 |             data -= np.mean(data, axis=0)\n62 |             data /= np.std(data, axis=0)\n63 |             kmeans.partial_fit(data)\n64 |             buffer = []\n65 |         if index % 100 == 0:\n66 |             print(\"Partial fit of %4i out of %i\" % (index, 6 * len(faces.images)))\n67 | \n68 | dt = time.time() - t0\n69 | print(\"done in %.2fs.\" % dt)\n70 | \n71 | # %%\n72 | # Plot the results\n73 | # ----------------\n74 | \n75 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"online\", \"online MiniBatchNMF\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:dict_learning_online`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 738 | def dict_learning_online(\n 739 |     X,\n 740 |     n_components=2,\n 741 |     *,\n 742 |     alpha=1,\n 743 |     n_iter=\"deprecated\",\n 744 |     max_iter=None,\n 745 |     return_code=True,\n 746 |     dict_init=None,\n 747 |     callback=None,\n 748 |     batch_size=\"warn\",\n 749 |     verbose=False,\n 750 |     shuffle=True,\n 751 |     n_jobs=None,\n 752 |     method=\"lars\",\n 753 |     iter_offset=\"deprecated\",\n 754 |     random_state=None,\n 755 |     return_inner_stats=\"deprecated\",\n 756 |     inner_stats=\"deprecated\",\n 757 |     return_n_iter=\"deprecated\",\n 758 |     positive_dict=False,\n 759 |     positive_code=False,\n 760 |     method_max_iter=1000,\n 761 |     tol=1e-3,\n 762 |     max_no_improvement=10,\n 763 | ):\n 764 |     \"\"\"Solves a dictionary learning matrix factorization problem online.\n 765 | \n 766 |     Finds the best dictionary and the corresponding sparse code for\n 767 |     approximating the data matrix X by solving::\n 768 | \n 769 |         (U^*, V^*) = argmin 0.5 || X - U V ||_Fro^2 + alpha * || U ||_1,1\n 770 |                      (U,V)\n 771 |                      with || V_k ||_2 = 1 for all  0 <= k < n_components\n 772 | \n 773 |     where V is the dictionary and U is the sparse code. ||.||_Fro stands for\n 774 |     the Frobenius norm and ||.||_1,1 stands for the entry-wise matrix norm\n 775 |     which is the sum of the absolute values of all the entries in the matrix.\n 776 |     This is accomplished by repeatedly iterating over mini-batches by slicing\n 777 |     the input data.\n 778 | \n 779 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <DictionaryLearning>`.\n 780 | \n 781 |     Parameters\n 782 |     ----------\n 783 |     X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 784 |         Data matrix.\n 785 | \n 786 |     n_components : int or None, default=2\n 787 |         Number of dictionary atoms to extract. If None, then ``n_components``\n 788 |         is set to ``n_features``.\n 789 | \n 790 |     alpha : float, default=1\n 791 |         Sparsity controlling parameter.\n 792 | \n 793 |     n_iter : int, default=100\n 794 |         Number of mini-batch iterations to perform.\n 795 | \n 796 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 797 |            `n_iter` is deprecated in 1.1 and will be removed in 1.3. Use\n 798 |            `max_iter` instead.\n 799 | \n 800 |     max_iter : int, default=None\n 801 |         Maximum number of iterations over the complete dataset before\n 802 |         stopping independently of any early stopping criterion heuristics.\n 803 |         If ``max_iter`` is not None, ``n_iter`` is ignored.\n 804 | \n 805 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1\n 806 | \n 807 |     return_code : bool, default=True\n 808 |         Whether to also return the code U or just the dictionary `V`.\n 809 | \n 810 |     dict_init : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 811 |         Initial value for the dictionary for warm restart scenarios.\n 812 | \n 813 |     callback : callable, default=None\n 814 |         A callable that gets invoked at the end of each iteration.\n 815 | \n 816 |     batch_size : int, default=3\n 817 |         The number of samples to take in each batch.\n 818 | \n 819 |     verbose : bool, default=False\n 820 |         To control the verbosity of the procedure.\n 821 | \n 822 |     shuffle : bool, default=True\n 823 |         Whether to shuffle the data before splitting it in batches.\n 824 | \n 825 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n 826 |         Number of parallel jobs to run.\n 827 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n 828 |         ``-1`` means using all processors. See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>`\n 829 |         for more details.\n 830 | \n 831 |     method : {'lars', 'cd'}, default='lars'\n 832 |         * `'lars'`: uses the least angle regression method to solve the lasso\n 833 |           problem (`linear_model.lars_path`);\n 834 |         * `'cd'`: uses the coordinate descent method to compute the\n 835 |           Lasso solution (`linear_model.Lasso`). Lars will be faster if\n 836 |           the estimated components are sparse.\n 837 | \n 838 |     iter_offset : int, default=0\n 839 |         Number of previous iterations completed on the dictionary used for\n 840 |         initialization.\n 841 | \n 842 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 843 |            `iter_offset` serves internal purpose only and will be removed in 1.3.\n 844 | \n 845 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n 846 |         Used for initializing the dictionary when ``dict_init`` is not\n 847 |         specified, randomly shuffling the data when ``shuffle`` is set to\n 848 |         ``True``, and updating the dictionary. Pass an int for reproducible\n 849 |         results across multiple function calls.\n 850 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n 851 | \n 852 |     return_inner_stats : bool, default=False\n 853 |         Return the inner statistics A (dictionary covariance) and B\n 854 |         (data approximation). Useful to restart the algorithm in an\n 855 |         online setting. If `return_inner_stats` is `True`, `return_code` is\n 856 |         ignored.\n 857 | \n 858 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 859 |            `return_inner_stats` serves internal purpose only and will be removed in 1.3.\n 860 | \n 861 |     inner_stats : tuple of (A, B) ndarrays, default=None\n 862 |         Inner sufficient statistics that are kept by the algorithm.\n 863 |         Passing them at initialization is useful in online settings, to\n 864 |         avoid losing the history of the evolution.\n 865 |         `A` `(n_components, n_components)` is the dictionary covariance matrix.\n 866 |         `B` `(n_features, n_components)` is the data approximation matrix.\n 867 | \n 868 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 869 |            `inner_stats` serves internal purpose only and will be removed in 1.3.\n 870 | \n 871 |     return_n_iter : bool, default=False\n 872 |         Whether or not to return the number of iterations.\n 873 | \n 874 |         .. deprecated:: 1.1\n 875 |            `return_n_iter` will be removed in 1.3 and n_iter will always be returned.\n 876 | \n 877 |     positive_dict : bool, default=False\n 878 |         Whether to enforce positivity when finding the dictionary.\n 879 | \n 880 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 881 | \n 882 |     positive_code : bool, default=False\n 883 |         Whether to enforce positivity when finding the code.\n 884 | \n 885 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 886 | \n 887 |     method_max_iter : int, default=1000\n 888 |         Maximum number of iterations to perform when solving the lasso problem.\n 889 | \n 890 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n 891 | \n 892 |     tol : float, default=1e-3\n 893 |         Control early stopping based on the norm of the differences in the\n 894 |         dictionary between 2 steps. Used only if `max_iter` is not None.\n 895 | \n 896 |         To disable early stopping based on changes in the dictionary, set\n 897 |         `tol` to 0.0.\n 898 | \n 899 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1\n 900 | \n 901 |     max_no_improvement : int, default=10\n 902 |         Control early stopping based on the consecutive number of mini batches\n 903 |         that does not yield an improvement on the smoothed cost function. Used only if\n 904 |         `max_iter` is not None.\n 905 | \n 906 |         To disable convergence detection based on cost function, set\n 907 |         `max_no_improvement` to None.\n 908 | \n 909 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1\n 910 | \n 911 |     Returns\n 912 |     -------\n 913 |     code : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components),\n 914 |         The sparse code (only returned if `return_code=True`).\n 915 | \n 916 |     dictionary : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features),\n 917 |         The solutions to the dictionary learning problem.\n 918 | \n 919 |     n_iter : int\n 920 |         Number of iterations run. Returned only if `return_n_iter` is\n 921 |         set to `True`.\n 922 | \n 923 |     See Also\n 924 |     --------\n 925 |     dict_learning\n 926 |     DictionaryLearning\n 927 |     MiniBatchDictionaryLearning\n 928 |     SparsePCA\n 929 |     MiniBatchSparsePCA\n 930 |     \"\"\"\n 931 |     deps = (return_n_iter, return_inner_stats, iter_offset, inner_stats)\n 932 |     if max_iter is not None and not all(arg == \"deprecated\" for arg in deps):\n 933 |         raise ValueError(\n 934 |             \"The following arguments are incompatible with 'max_iter': \"\n 935 |             \"return_n_iter, return_inner_stats, iter_offset, inner_stats\"\n 936 |         )\n 937 | \n 938 |     iter_off