\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also search for the `_download_data_to_bunch` function since it's involved in downloading the data:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_download_data_to_bunch']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"_download_data_to_bunch\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also look at the data loading utilities:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['load_arff', '_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\n##Searching for term \"load_arff\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"RCV1 dataset.\n  2 | \n  3 | The dataset page is available at\n  4 | \n  5 |     http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/\n  6 | \"\"\"\n  7 | \n  8 | # Author: Tom Dupre la Tour\n  9 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 10 | \n 11 | import logging\n 12 | \n 13 | from os import remove, makedirs\n 14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, join\n 15 | from gzip import GzipFile\n 16 | \n 17 | import numpy as np\n 18 | import scipy.sparse as sp\n 19 | import joblib\n 20 | \n 21 | from . import get_data_home\n 22 | from ._base import _pkl_filepath\n 23 | from ._base import _fetch_remote\n 24 | from ._base import RemoteFileMetadata\n 25 | from ._svmlight_format_io import load_svmlight_files\n 26 | from ..utils import shuffle as shuffle_\n 27 | from ..utils import Bunch\n 28 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n 29 | \n 30 | \n 31 | # The original vectorized data can be found at:\n 32 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz\n 33 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz\n 34 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz\n 35 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz\n 36 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz\n 37 | # while the original stemmed token files can be found\n 38 | # in the README, section B.12.i.:\n 39 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/lyrl2004_rcv1v2_README.htm\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 40 | XY_METADATA = (\n 41 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 42 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976069',\n 43 |         checksum=('ed40f7e418d10484091b059703eeb95a'\n 44 |                   'e3199fe042891dcec4be6696b9968374'),\n 45 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz'),\n 46 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 47 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976066',\n 48 |         checksum=('87700668ae45d45d5ca1ef6ae9bd81ab'\n 49 |                   '0f5ec88cc95dcef9ae7838f727a13aa6'),\n 50 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz'),\n 51 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 52 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976063',\n 53 |         checksum=('48143ac703cbe33299f7ae9f4995db4'\n 54 |                   '9a258690f60e5debbff8995c34841c7f5'),\n 55 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz'),\n 56 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 57 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976060',\n 58 |         checksum=('dfcb0d658311481523c6e6ca0c3f5a3'\n 59 |                   'e1d3d12cde5d7a8ce629a9006ec7dbb39'),\n 60 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz'),\n 61 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 62 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976057',\n 63 |         checksum=('5468f656d0ba7a83afc7ad44841cf9a5'\n 64 |                   '3048a5c083eedc005dcdb5cc768924ae'),\n 65 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz')\n 66 | )\n 67 | \n 68 | # The original data can be found at:\n 69 | # http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a08-topic-qrels/rcv1-v2.topics.qrels.gz\n 70 | TOPICS_METADATA = RemoteFileMetadata(\n 71 |     url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976048',\n 72 |     checksum=('2a98e5e5d8b770bded93afc8930d882'\n 73 |               '99474317fe14181aee1466cc754d0d1c1'),\n 74 |     filename='rcv1v2.topics.qrels.gz')\n 75 | \n 76 | logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_base.py:load_files`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 85 | def load_files(container_path, *, description=None, categories=None,\n 86 |                load_content=True, shuffle=True, encoding=None,\n 87 |                decode_error='strict', random_state=0):\n 88 |     \"\"\"Load text files with categories as subfolder names.\n 89 | \n 90 |     Individual samples are assumed to be files stored a two levels folder\n 91 |     structure such as the following:\n 92 | \n 93 |         container_folder/\n 94 |             category_1_folder/\n 95 |                 file_1.txt\n 96 |                 file_2.txt\n 97 |                 ...\n 98 |                 file_42.txt\n 99 |             category_2_folder/\n100 |                 file_43.txt\n101 |                 file_44.txt\n102 |                 ...\n103 | \n104 |     The folder names are used as supervised signal label names. The individual\n105 |     file names are not important.\n106 | \n107 |     This function does not try to extract features into a numpy array or scipy\n108 |     sparse matrix. In addition, if load_content is false it does not try to\n109 |     load the files in memory.\n110 | \n111 |     To use text files in a scikit-learn classification or clustering algorithm,\n112 |     you will need to use the :mod`~sklearn.feature_extraction.text` module to\n113 |     build a feature extraction transformer that suits your problem.\n114 | \n115 |     If you set load_content=True, you should also specify the encoding of the\n116 |     text using the 'encoding' parameter. For many modern text files, 'utf-8'\n117 |     will be the correct encoding. If you leave encoding equal to None, then the\n118 |     content will be made of bytes instead of Unicode, and you will not be able\n119 |     to use most functions in :mod:`~sklearn.feature_extraction.text`.\n120 | \n121 |     Similar feature extractors should be built for other kind of unstructured\n122 |     data input such as images, audio, video, ...\n123 | \n124 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <datasets>`.\n125 | \n126 |     Parameters\n127 |     ----------\n128 |     container_path : str or unicode\n129 |         Path to the main folder holding one subfolder per category\n130 | \n131 |     description : str or unicode, default=None\n132 |         A paragraph describing the characteristic of the dataset: its source,\n133 |         reference, etc.\n134 | \n135 |     categories : list of str, default=None\n136 |         If None (default), load all the categories. If not None, list of\n137 |         category names to load (other categories ignored).\n138 | \n139 |     load_content : bool, default=True\n140 |         Whether to load or not the content of the different files. If true a\n141 |         'data' attribute containing the text information is present in the data\n142 |         structure returned. If not, a filenames attribute gives the path to the\n143 |         files.\n144 | \n145 |     shuffle : bool, default=True\n146 |         Whether or not to shuffle the data: might be important for models that\n147 |         make the assumption that the samples are independent and identically\n148 |         distributed (i.i.d.), such as stochastic gradient descent.\n149 | \n150 |     encoding : str, default=None\n151 |         If None, do not try to decode the content of the files (e.g. for images\n152 |         or other non-text content). If not None, encoding to use to decode text\n153 |         files to Unicode if load_content is True.\n154 | \n155 |     decode_error : {'strict', 'ignore', 'replace'}, default='strict'\n156 |         Instruction on what to do if a byte sequence is given to analyze that\n157 |         contains characters not of the given `encoding`. Passed as keyword\n158 |         argument 'errors' to bytes.decode.\n159 | \n160 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=0\n161 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n162 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n163 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n164 | \n165 |     Returns\n166 |     -------\n167 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n168 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n169 | \n170 |         data : list of str\n171 |             Only present when `load_content=True`.\n172 |             The raw text data to learn.\n173 |         target : ndarray\n174 |             The target labels (integer index).\n175 |         target_names : list\n176 |             The names of target classes.\n177 |         DESCR : str\n178 |             The full description of the dataset.\n179 |         filenames: ndarray\n180 |             The filenames holding the dataset.\n181 |     \"\"\"\n182 |     target = []\n183 |     target_names = []\n184 |     filenames = []\n185 | \n186 |     folders = [f for f in sorted(listdir(container_path))\n187 |                if isdir(join(container_path, f))]\n188 | \n189 |     if categories is not None:\n190 |         folders = [f for f in folders if f in categories]\n191 | \n192 |     for label, folder in enumerate(folders):\n193 |         target_names.append(folder)\n194 |         folder_path = join(container_path, folder)\n195 |         documents = [join(folder_path, d)\n196 |                      for d in sorted(listdir(folder_path))]\n197 |         target.extend(len(documents) * [label])\n198 |         filenames.extend(documents)\n199 | \n200 |     # convert to array for fancy indexing\n201 |     filenames = np.array(filenames)\n202 |     target = np.array(target)\n203 | \n204 |     if shuffle:\n205 |         random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n206 |         indices = np.arange(filenames.shape[0])\n207 |         random_state.shuffle(indices)\n208 |         filenames = filenames[indices]\n209 |         target = target[indices]\n210 | \n211 |     if load_content:\n212 |         data = []\n213 |         for filename in filenames:\n214 |             with open(filename, 'rb') as f:\n215 |                 data.append(f.read())\n216 |         if encoding is not None:\n217 |             data = [d.decode(encoding, decode_error) for d in data]\n218 |         return Bunch(data=data,\n219 |                      filenames=filenames,\n220 |                      target_names=target_names,\n221 |                      target=target,\n222 |                      DESCR=description)\n223 | \n224 |     return Bunch(filenames=filenames,\n225 |                  target_names=target_names,\n226 |                  target=target,\n227 |                  DESCR=description)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 80 | def fetch_rcv1(*, data_home=None, subset='all', download_if_missing=True,\n 81 |                random_state=None, shuffle=False, return_X_y=False):\n 82 |     \"\"\"Load the RCV1 multilabel dataset (classification).\n 83 | \n 84 |     Download it if necessary.\n 85 | \n 86 |     Version: RCV1-v2, vectors, full sets, topics multilabels.\n 87 | \n 88 |     =================   =====================\n 89 |     Classes                               103\n 90 |     Samples total                      804414\n 91 |     Dimensionality                      47236\n 92 |     Features            real, between 0 and 1\n 93 |     =================   =====================\n 94 | \n 95 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rcv1_dataset>`.\n 96 | \n 97 |     .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 98 | \n 99 |     Parameters\n100 |     ----------\n101 |     data_home : str, default=None\n102 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the datasets. By default\n103 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n104 | \n105 |     subset : {'train', 'test', 'all'}, default='all'\n106 |         Select the dataset to load: 'train' for the training set\n107 |         (23149 samples), 'test' for the test set (781265 samples),\n108 |         'all' for both, with the training samples first if shuffle is False.\n109 |         This follows the official LYRL2004 chronological split.\n110 | \n111 |     download_if_missing : bool, default=True\n112 |         If False, raise a IOError if the data is not locally available\n113 |         instead of trying to download the data from the source site.\n114 | \n115 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n116 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n117 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n118 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n119 | \n120 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n121 |         Whether to shuffle dataset.\n122 | \n123 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n124 |         If True, returns ``(dataset.data, dataset.target)`` instead of a Bunch\n125 |         object. See below for more information about the `dataset.data` and\n126 |         `dataset.target` object.\n127 | \n128 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n129 | \n130 |     Returns\n131 |     -------\n132 |     dataset : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n133 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n134 | \n135 |         data : scipy csr array, dtype np.float64, shape (804414, 47236)\n136 |             The array has 0.16% of non zero values.\n137 |         target : scipy csr array, dtype np.uint8, shape (804414, 103)\n138 |             Each sample has a value of 1 in its categories, and 0 in others.\n139 |             The array has 3.15% of non zero values.\n140 |         sample_id : numpy array, dtype np.uint32, shape (804414,)\n141 |             Identification number of each sample, as ordered in dataset.data.\n142 |         target_names : numpy array, dtype object, length (103)\n143 |             Names of each target (RCV1 topics), as ordered in dataset.target.\n144 |         DESCR : string\n145 |             Description of the RCV1 dataset.\n146 | \n147 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n150 |     \"\"\"\n151 |     N_SAMPLES = 804414\n152 |     N_FEATURES = 47236\n153 |     N_CATEGORIES = 103\n154 |     N_TRAIN = 23149\n155 | \n156 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n157 |     rcv1_dir = join(data_home, \"RCV1\")\n158 |     if download_if_missing:\n159 |         if not exists(rcv1_dir):\n160 |             makedirs(rcv1_dir)\n161 | \n162 |     samples_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"samples.pkl\")\n163 |     sample_id_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_id.pkl\")\n164 |     sample_topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_topics.pkl\")\n165 |     topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"topics_names.pkl\")\n166 | \n167 |     # load data (X) and sample_id\n168 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(samples_path) or\n169 |                                 not exists(sample_id_path)):\n170 |         files = []\n171 |         for each in XY_METADATA:\n172 |             logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % each.url)\n173 |             file_path = _fetch_remote(each, dirname=rcv1_dir)\n174 |             files.append(GzipFile(filename=file_path))\n175 | \n176 |         Xy = load_svmlight_files(files, n_features=N_FEATURES)\n177 | \n178 |         # Training data is before testing data\n179 |         X = sp.vstack([Xy[8], Xy[0], Xy[2], Xy[4], Xy[6]]).tocsr()\n180 |         sample_id = np.hstack((Xy[9], Xy[1], Xy[3], Xy[5], Xy[7]))\n181 |         sample_id = sample_id.astype(np.uint32, copy=False)\n182 | \n183 |         joblib.dump(X, samples_path, compress=9)\n184 |         joblib.dump(sample_id, sample_id_path, compress=9)\n185 | \n186 |         # delete archives\n187 |         for f in files:\n188 |             f.close()\n189 |             remove(f.name)\n190 |     else:\n191 |         X = joblib.load(samples_path)\n192 |         sample_id = joblib.load(sample_id_path)\n193 | \n194 |     # load target (y), categories, and sample_id_bis\n195 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(sample_topics_path) or\n196 |                                 not exists(topics_path)):\n197 |         logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % TOPICS_METADATA.url)\n198 |         topics_archive_path = _fetch_remote(TOPICS_METADATA,\n199 |                                             dirname=rcv1_dir)\n200 | \n201 |         # parse the target file\n202 |         n_cat = -1\n203 |         n_doc = -1\n204 |         doc_previous = -1\n205 |         y = np.zeros((N_SAMPLES, N_CATEGORIES), dtype=np.uint8)\n206 |         sample_id_bis = np.zeros(N_SAMPLES, dtype=np.int32)\n207 |         category_names = {}\n208 |         with GzipFile(filename=topics_archive_path, mode='rb') as f:\n209 |             for line in f:\n210 |                 line_components = line.decode(\"ascii\").split(\" \")\n211 |                 if len(line_components) == 3:\n212 |                     cat, doc, _ = line_components\n213 |                     if cat not in category_names:\n214 |                         n_cat += 1\n215 |                         category_names[cat] = n_cat\n216 | \n217 |                     doc = int(doc)\n218 |                     if doc != doc_previous:\n219 |                         doc_previous = doc\n220 |                         n_doc += 1\n221 |                         sample_id_bis[n_doc] = doc\n222 |                     y[n_doc, category_names[cat]] = 1\n223 | \n224 |         # delete archive\n225 |         remove(topics_archive_path)\n226 | \n227 |         # Samples in X are ordered with sample_id,\n228 |         # whereas in y, they are ordered with sample_id_bis.\n229 |         permutation = _find_permutation(sample_id_bis, sample_id)\n230 |         y = y[permutation, :]\n231 | \n232 |         # save category names in a list, with same order than y\n233 |         categories = np.empty(N_CATEGORIES, dtype=object)\n234 |         for k in category_names.keys():\n235 |             categories[category_names[k]] = k\n236 | \n237 |         # reorder categories in lexicographic order\n238 |         order = np.argsort(categories)\n239 |         categories = categories[order]\n240 |         y = sp.csr_matrix(y[:, order])\n241 | \n242 |         joblib.dump(y, sample_topics_path, compress=9)\n243 |         joblib.dump(categories, topics_path, compress=9)\n244 |     else:\n245 |         y = joblib.load(sample_topics_path)\n246 |         categories = joblib.load(topics_path)\n247 | \n248 |     if subset == 'all':\n249 |         pass\n250 |     elif subset == 'train':\n251 |         X = X[:N_TRAIN, :]\n252 |         y = y[:N_TRAIN, :]\n253 |         sample_id = sample_id[:N_TRAIN]\n254 |     elif subset == 'test':\n255 |         X = X[N_TRAIN:, :]\n256 |         y = y[N_TRAIN:, :]\n257 |         sample_id = sample_id[N_TRAIN:]\n258 |     else:\n259 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown subset parameter. Got '%s' instead of one\"\n260 |                          \" of ('all', 'train', test')\" % subset)\n261 | \n262 |     if shuffle:\n263 |         X, y, sample_id = shuffle_(X, y, sample_id, random_state=random_state)\n264 | \n265 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n266 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'rcv1.rst')) as rst_file:\n267 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n268 | \n269 |     if return_X_y:\n270 |         return X, y\n271 | \n272 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, sample_id=sample_id,\n273 |                  target_names=categories, DESCR=fdescr)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/externals/_arff.py:load`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1063 | def load(fp, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1064 |     '''Load a file-like object containing the ARFF document and convert it into\n1065 |     a Python object.\n1066 | \n1067 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1068 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1069 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1070 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1071 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1072 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1073 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1074 |         progressively`_.\n1075 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1076 |      '''\n1077 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1078 |     return decoder.decode(fp, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1079 |                           return_type=return_type)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1344 | def _check_fit_params(X, fit_params, indices=None):\n1345 |     \"\"\"Check and validate the parameters passed during `fit`.\n1346 | \n1347 |     Parameters\n1348 |     ----------\n1349 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1350 |         Data array.\n1351 | \n1352 |     fit_params : dict\n1353 |         Dictionary containing the parameters passed at fit.\n1354 | \n1355 |     indices : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1356 |         Indices to be selected if the parameter has the same size as `X`.\n1357 | \n1358 |     Returns\n1359 |     -------\n1360 |     fit_params_validated : dict\n1361 |         Validated parameters. We ensure that the values support indexing.\n1362 |     \"\"\"\n1363 |     from . import _safe_indexing\n1364 |     fit_params_validated = {}\n1365 |     for param_key, param_value in fit_params.items():\n1366 |         if (not _is_arraylike(param_value) or\n1367 |                 _num_samples(param_value) != _num_samples(X)):\n1368 |             # Non-indexable pass-through (for now for backward-compatibility).\n1369 |             # https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/15805\n1370 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = param_value\n1371 |         else:\n1372 |             # Any other fit_params should support indexing\n1373 |             # (e.g. for cross-validation).\n1374 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _make_indexable(param_value)\n1375 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _safe_indexing(\n1376 |                 fit_params_validated[param_key], indices\n1377 |             )\n1378 | \n1379 |     return fit_params_validated\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"load_arff\", \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let's also look at the validation utilities we might need:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['get_data_home', 'warn']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:_VectorizerMixin._warn_for_unused_params\nsklearn/exceptions.py:UndefinedMetricWarning\nsklearn/exceptions.py:NonBLASDotWarning\nsklearn/exceptions.py:EfficiencyWarning\nsklearn/exceptions.py:SkipTestWarning\nsklearn/exceptions.py:FitFailedWarning\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:column_or_1d\nsklearn/metrics/_classification.py:_prf_divide\n##Searching for term \"get_data_home\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_base.py:get_data_home`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `get_data_home`.\n```\n30 | def get_data_home(data_home=None) -> str:\n31 |     \"\"\"Return the path of the scikit-learn data dir.\n32 | \n33 |     This folder is used by some large dataset loaders to avoid downloading the\n34 |     data several times.\n35 | \n36 |     By default the data dir is set to a folder named 'scikit_learn_data' in the\n37 |     user home folder.\n38 | \n39 |     Alternatively, it can be set by the 'SCIKIT_LEARN_DATA' environment\n40 |     variable or programmatically by giving an explicit folder path. The '~'\n41 |     symbol is expanded to the user home folder.\n42 | \n43 |     If the folder does not already exist, it is automatically created.\n44 | \n45 |     Parameters\n46 |     ----------\n47 |     data_home : str | None\n48 |         The path to scikit-learn data dir.\n49 |     \"\"\"\n50 |     if data_home is None:\n51 |         data_home = environ.get('SCIKIT_LEARN_DATA',\n52 |                                 join('~', 'scikit_learn_data'))\n53 |     data_home = expanduser(data_home)\n54 |     if not exists(data_home):\n55 |         makedirs(data_home)\n56 |     return data_home\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"warn\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:_warn_prf`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1230 | def _warn_prf(average, modifier, msg_start, result_size):\n1231 |     axis0, axis1 = 'sample', 'label'\n1232 |     if average == 'samples':\n1233 |         axis0, axis1 = axis1, axis0\n1234 |     msg = ('{0} ill-defined and being set to 0.0 {{0}} '\n1235 |            'no {1} {2}s. Use `zero_division` parameter to control'\n1236 |            ' this behavior.'.format(msg_start, modifier, axis0))\n1237 |     if result_size == 1:\n1238 |         msg = msg.format('due to')\n1239 |     else:\n1240 |         msg = msg.format('in {0}s with'.format(axis1))\n1241 |     warnings.warn(msg, UndefinedMetricWarning, stacklevel=2)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:_VectorizerMixin._warn_for_unused_params`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n481 | def _warn_for_unused_params(self):\n482 | \n483 |         if self.tokenizer is not None and self.token_pattern is not None:\n484 |             warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'token_pattern' will not be used\"\n485 |                           \" since 'tokenizer' is not None'\")\n486 | \n487 |         if self.preprocessor is not None and callable(self.analyzer):\n488 |             warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'preprocessor' will not be used\"\n489 |                           \" since 'analyzer' is callable'\")\n490 | \n491 |         if (self.ngram_range != (1, 1) and self.ngram_range is not None\n492 |                 and callable(self.analyzer)):\n493 |             warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'ngram_range' will not be used\"\n494 |                           \" since 'analyzer' is callable'\")\n495 |         if self.analyzer != 'word' or callable(self.analyzer):\n496 |             if self.stop_words is not None:\n497 |                 warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'stop_words' will not be used\"\n498 |                               \" since 'analyzer' != 'word'\")\n499 |             if self.token_pattern is not None and \\\n500 |                self.token_pattern != r\"(?u)\\b\\w\\w+\\b\":\n501 |                 warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'token_pattern' will not be used\"\n502 |                               \" since 'analyzer' != 'word'\")\n503 |             if self.tokenizer is not None:\n504 |                 warnings.warn(\"The parameter 'tokenizer' will not be used\"\n505 |                               \" since 'analyzer' != 'word'\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/svm/_base.py:BaseLibSVM._warn_from_fit_status`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n243 | def _warn_from_fit_status(self):\n244 |         assert self.fit_status_ in (0, 1)\n245 |         if self.fit_status_ == 1:\n246 |             warnings.warn('Solver terminated early (max_iter=%i).'\n247 |                           '  Consider pre-processing your data with'\n248 |                           ' StandardScaler or MinMaxScaler.'\n249 |                           % self.max_iter, ConvergenceWarning)\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/exceptions.py:EfficiencyWarning`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n89 | class EfficiencyWarning(UserWarning):\n90 |     \"\"\"Warning used to notify the user of inefficient computation.\n91 | \n92 |     This warning notifies the user that the efficiency may not be optimal due\n93 |     to some reason which may be included as a part of the warning message.\n94 |     This may be subclassed into a more specific Warning class.\n95 | \n96 |     .. versionadded:: 0.18\n97 |     \"\"\"\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/exceptions.py:NonBLASDotWarning`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n112 | class NonBLASDotWarning(EfficiencyWarning):\n113 |     \"\"\"Warning used when the dot operation does not use BLAS.\n114 | \n115 |     This warning is used to notify the user that BLAS was not used for dot\n116 |     operation and hence the efficiency may be affected.\n117 | \n118 |     .. versionchanged:: 0.18\n119 |        Moved from sklearn.utils.validation, extends EfficiencyWarning.\n120 |     \"\"\"\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/exceptions.py:SkipTestWarning`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n123 | class SkipTestWarning(UserWarning):\n124 |     \"\"\"Warning class used to notify the user of a test that was skipped.\n125 | \n126 |     For example, one of the estimator checks requires a pandas import.\n127 |     If the pandas package cannot be imported, the test will be skip