\"\"\n 2 | =======================\n 3 | Bar chart on polar axis\n 4 | =======================\n 5 | \n 6 | Demo of bar plot on a polar axis.\n 7 | \"\"\"\n 8 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 9 | import numpy as np\n10 | \n11 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n12 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n13 | \n14 | # Compute pie slices\n15 | N = 20\n16 | theta = np.linspace(0.0, 2 * np.pi, N, endpoint=False)\n17 | radii = 10 * np.random.rand(N)\n18 | width = np.pi / 4 * np.random.rand(N)\n19 | colors = plt.cm.viridis(radii / 10.)\n20 | \n21 | ax = plt.subplot(projection='polar')\n22 | ax.bar(theta, radii, width=width, bottom=0.0, color=colors, alpha=0.5)\n23 | \n24 | plt.show()\n25 | \n26 | # %%\n27 | #\n28 | # .. admonition:: References\n29 | #\n30 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n31 | #    in this example:\n32 | #\n33 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar` / `matplotlib.pyplot.bar`\n34 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar`\n35 | #\n36 | # .. tags::\n37 | #\n38 | #    plot-type: pie\n39 | #    plot-type: bar\n40 | #    level: beginner\n41 | #    purpose: showcase\n42 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/mplot3d/bars3d.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ========================================\n 3 | Create 2D bar graphs in different planes\n 4 | ========================================\n 5 | \n 6 | Demonstrates making a 3D plot which has 2D bar graphs projected onto\n 7 | planes y=0, y=1, etc.\n 8 | \"\"\"\n 9 | \n10 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n11 | import numpy as np\n12 | \n13 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n14 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n15 | \n16 | \n17 | fig = plt.figure()\n18 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='3d')\n19 | \n20 | colors = ['r', 'g', 'b', 'y']\n21 | yticks = [3, 2, 1, 0]\n22 | for c, k in zip(colors, yticks):\n23 |     # Generate the random data for the y=k 'layer'.\n24 |     xs = np.arange(20)\n25 |     ys = np.random.rand(20)\n26 | \n27 |     # You can provide either a single color or an array with the same length as\n28 |     # xs and ys. To demonstrate this, we color the first bar of each set cyan.\n29 |     cs = [c] * len(xs)\n30 |     cs[0] = 'c'\n31 | \n32 |     # Plot the bar graph given by xs and ys on the plane y=k with 80% opacity.\n33 |     ax.bar(xs, ys, zs=k, zdir='y', color=cs, alpha=0.8)\n34 | \n35 | ax.set_xlabel('X')\n36 | ax.set_ylabel('Y')\n37 | ax.set_zlabel('Z')\n38 | \n39 | # On the y-axis let's only label the discrete values that we have data for.\n40 | ax.set_yticks(yticks)\n41 | \n42 | plt.show()\n43 | \n44 | # %%\n45 | # .. tags::\n46 | #    plot-type: 3D, plot-type: bar,\n47 | #    styling: color,\n48 | #    level: beginner\n49 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/pie_and_polar_charts/polar_error_caps.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | =================================\n 3 | Error bar rendering on polar axis\n 4 | =================================\n 5 | \n 6 | Demo of error bar plot in polar coordinates.\n 7 | Theta error bars are curved lines ended with caps oriented towards the\n 8 | center.\n 9 | Radius error bars are straight lines oriented towards center with\n10 | perpendicular caps.\n11 | \"\"\"\n12 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n13 | import numpy as np\n14 | \n15 | theta = np.arange(0, 2 * np.pi, np.pi / 4)\n16 | r = theta / np.pi / 2 + 0.5\n17 | \n18 | fig = plt.figure(figsize=(10, 10))\n19 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n20 | ax.errorbar(theta, r, xerr=0.25, yerr=0.1, capsize=7, fmt=\"o\", c=\"seagreen\")\n21 | ax.set_title(\"Pretty polar error bars\")\n22 | plt.show()\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | # Please acknowledge that large theta error bars will be overlapping.\n26 | # This may reduce readability of the output plot. See example figure below:\n27 | \n28 | fig = plt.figure(figsize=(10, 10))\n29 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n30 | ax.errorbar(theta, r, xerr=5.25, yerr=0.1, capsize=7, fmt=\"o\", c=\"darkred\")\n31 | ax.set_title(\"Overlapping theta error bars\")\n32 | plt.show()\n33 | \n34 | # %%\n35 | # On the other hand, large radius error bars will never overlap, they just\n36 | # lead to unwanted scale in the data, reducing the displayed range.\n37 | \n38 | fig = plt.figure(figsize=(10, 10))\n39 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n40 | ax.errorbar(theta, r, xerr=0.25, yerr=10.1, capsize=7, fmt=\"o\", c=\"orangered\")\n41 | ax.set_title(\"Large radius error bars\")\n42 | plt.show()\n43 | \n44 | \n45 | # %%\n46 | #\n47 | # .. admonition:: References\n48 | #\n49 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n50 | #    in this example:\n51 | #\n52 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.errorbar` / `matplotlib.pyplot.errorbar`\n53 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar`\n54 | #\n55 | # .. tags::\n56 | #\n57 | #    component: error\n58 | #    plot-type: errorbar\n59 | #    plot-type: polar\n60 | #    level: beginner\n61 | \n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.barh`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2638 | def barh(self, y, width, height=0.8, left=None, *, align=\"center\",\n2639 |              data=None, **kwargs):\n2640 |         r\"\"\"\n2641 |         Make a horizontal bar plot.\n2642 | \n2643 |         The bars are positioned at *y* with the given *align*\\ment. Their\n2644 |         dimensions are given by *width* and *height*. The horizontal baseline\n2645 |         is *left* (default 0).\n2646 | \n2647 |         Many parameters can take either a single value applying to all bars\n2648 |         or a sequence of values, one for each bar.\n2649 | \n2650 |         Parameters\n2651 |         ----------\n2652 |         y : float or array-like\n2653 |             The y coordinates of the bars. See also *align* for the\n2654 |             alignment of the bars to the coordinates.\n2655 | \n2656 |         width : float or array-like\n2657 |             The width(s) of the bars.\n2658 | \n2659 |             Note that if *left* has units (e.g. datetime), *width* should be in\n2660 |             units that are a difference from the value of *left* (e.g. timedelta).\n2661 | \n2662 |         height : float or array-like, default: 0.8\n2663 |             The heights of the bars.\n2664 | \n2665 |             Note that if *y* has units (e.g. datetime), then *height* should be in\n2666 |             units that are a difference (e.g. timedelta) around the *y* values.\n2667 | \n2668 |         left : float or array-like, default: 0\n2669 |             The x coordinates of the left side(s) of the bars.\n2670 | \n2671 |             Note that if *left* has units, then the x-axis will get a Locator and\n2672 |             Formatter appropriate for the units (e.g. dates, or categorical).\n2673 | \n2674 |         align : {'center', 'edge'}, default: 'center'\n2675 |             Alignment of the base to the *y* coordinates*:\n2676 | \n2677 |             - 'center': Center the bars on the *y* positions.\n2678 |             - 'edge': Align the bottom edges of the bars with the *y*\n2679 |               positions.\n2680 | \n2681 |             To align the bars on the top edge pass a negative *height* and\n2682 |             ``align='edge'``.\n2683 | \n2684 |         Returns\n2685 |         -------\n2686 |         `.BarContainer`\n2687 |             Container with all the bars and optionally errorbars.\n2688 | \n2689 |         Other Parameters\n2690 |         ----------------\n2691 |         color : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2692 |             The colors of the bar faces.\n2693 | \n2694 |         edgecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2695 |             The colors of the bar edges.\n2696 | \n2697 |         linewidth : float or array-like, optional\n2698 |             Width of the bar edge(s). If 0, don't draw edges.\n2699 | \n2700