the object to a JSON string.\n2337 | \n2338 |         Note NaN's and None will be converted to null and datetime objects\n2339 |         will be converted to UNIX timestamps.\n2340 | \n2341 |         Parameters\n2342 |         ----------\n2343 |         path_or_buf : str, path object, file-like object, or None, default None\n2344 |             String, path object (implementing os.PathLike[str]), or file-like\n2345 |             object implementing a write() function. If None, the result is\n2346 |             returned as a string.\n2347 |         orient : str\n2348 |             Indication of expected JSON string format.\n2349 | \n2350 |             * Series:\n2351 | \n2352 |                 - default is 'index'\n2353 |                 - allowed values are: {{'split', 'records', 'index', 'table'}}.\n2354 | \n2355 |             * DataFrame:\n2356 | \n2357 |                 - default is 'columns'\n2358 |                 - allowed values are: {{'split', 'records', 'index', 'columns',\n2359 |                   'values', 'table'}}.\n2360 | \n2361 |             * The format of the JSON string:\n2362 | \n2363 |                 - 'split' : dict like {{'index' -> [index], 'columns' -> [columns],\n2364 |                   'data' -> [values]}}\n2365 |                 - 'records' : list like [{{column -> value}}, ... , {{column -> value}}]\n2366 |                 - 'index' : dict like {{index -> {{column -> value}}}}\n2367 |                 - 'columns' : dict like {{column -> {{index -> value}}}}\n2368 |                 - 'values' : just the values array\n2369 |                 - 'table' : dict like {{'schema': {{schema}}, 'data': {{data}}}}\n2370 | \n2371 |                 Describing the data, where data component is like ``orient='records'``.\n2372 | \n2373 |         date_format : {{None, 'epoch', 'iso'}}\n2374 |             Type of date conversion. 'epoch' = epoch milliseconds,\n2375 |             'iso' = ISO8601. The default depends on the `orient`. For\n2376 |             ``orient='table'``, the default is 'iso'. For all other orients,\n2377 |             the default is 'epoch'.\n2378 | \n2379 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n2380 |                 'epoch' date format is deprecated and will be removed in a future\n2381 |                 version, please use 'iso' instead.\n2382 | \n2383 |         double_precision : int, default 10\n2384 |             The number of decimal places to use when encoding\n2385 |             floating point values. The possible maximal value is 15.\n2386 |             Passing double_precision greater than 15 will raise a ValueError.\n2387 |         force_ascii : bool, default True\n2388 |             Force encoded string to be ASCII.\n2389 |         date_unit : str, default 'ms' (milliseconds)\n2390 |             The time unit to encode to, governs timestamp and ISO8601\n2391 |             precision.  One of 's', 'ms', 'us', 'ns' for second, millisecond,\n2392 |             microsecond, and nanosecond respectively.\n2393 |         default_handler : callable, default None\n2394 |             Handler to call if object cannot otherwise be converted to a\n2395 |             suitable format for JSON. Should receive a single argument which is\n2396 |             the object to convert and return a serialisable object.\n2397 |         lines : bool, default False\n2398 |             If 'orient' is 'records' write out line-delimited json format. Will\n2399 |             throw ValueError if incorrect 'orient' since others are not\n2400 |             list-like.\n2401 |         {compression_options}\n2402 | \n2403 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0 Zstandard support.\n2404 | \n2405 |         index : bool or None, default None\n2406 |             The index is only used when 'orient' is 'split', 'index', 'column',\n2407 |             or 'table'. Of these, 'index' and 'column' do not support\n2408 |             `index=False`. The string 'index' as a column name with empty :class:`Index`\n2409 |             or if it