            Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n791 | \n792 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally\n793 |               spaced. This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n794 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n795 |               given length of interval.\n796 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n797 |             * 'pad': Fill in NaNs using existing values.\n798 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic',\n799 |               'barycentric', 'polynomial': Passed to\n800 |               `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n801 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n802 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n803 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n804 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n805 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n806 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n807 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n808 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n809 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n810 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n811 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n812 | \n813 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n814 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n815 |             and defaults to 0.\n816 |         limit : int, optional\n817 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n818 |             0.\n819 |         inplace : bool, default False\n820 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n821 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, Optional\n822 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n823 | \n824 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n825 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n826 |             restriction.\n827 | \n828 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n829 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n830 |               (interpolate).\n831 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n832 | \n833 |         downcast : optional, 'infer' or None, defaults to None\n834 |             Downcast dtypes if possible.\n835 | \n836 |             .. deprecated:: 2.1.0\n837 | \n838 |         **kwargs : optional\n839 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n840 | \n841 |         Returns\n842 |         -------\n843 |         DataFrame or Series\n844 |             Interpolated values at the specified freq.\n845 | \n846 |         See Also\n847 |         --------\n848 |         core.resample.Resampler.asfreq: Return the values at the new freq,\n849 |             essentially a reindex.\n850 |         DataFrame.interpolate: Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n851 |         DataFrame.bfill : Backward fill NaN values in the resampled data.\n852 |         DataFrame.ffill : Forward fill NaN values.\n853 | \n854 |         Notes\n855 |         -----\n856 |         For high-frequent or non-equidistant time-series with timestamps\n857 |         the reindexing followed by interpolation may lead to information loss\n858 |         as shown in the last example.\n859 | \n860 |         Examples\n861 |         --------\n862 | \n863 |         >>> start = \"2023-03-01T07:00:00\"\n864 |         >>> timesteps = pd.date_range(start, periods=5, freq=\"s\")\n865 |         >>> series = pd.Series(data=[1, -1, 2, 1, 3], index=timesteps)\n866 |         >>> series\n867 |         2023-03-01