3-03-01 07:00:02.800    1.2\n911 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.200    1.4\n912 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.600    2.2\n913 |         2023-03-01 07:00:04.000    3.0\n914 |         Freq: 400ms, dtype: float64\n915 | \n916 |         Note that the series correctly decreases between two anchors\n917 |         ``07:00:00`` and ``07:00:02``.\n918 |         \"\"\"\n919 |         assert downcast is lib.no_default  # just checking coverage\n920 |         result = self._upsample(\"asfreq\")\n921 | \n922 |         # If the original data has timestamps which are not aligned with the\n923 |         # target timestamps, we need to add those points back to the data frame\n924 |         # that is supposed to be interpolated. This does not work with\n925 |         # PeriodIndex, so we skip this case. GH#21351\n926 |         obj = self._selected_obj\n927 |         is_period_index = isinstance(obj.index, PeriodIndex)\n928 | \n929 |         # Skip this step for PeriodIndex\n930 |         if not is_period_index:\n931 |             final_index = result.index\n932 |             if isinstance(final_index, MultiIndex):\n933 |                 raise NotImplementedError(\n934 |                     \"Direct interpolation of MultiIndex data frames is not \"\n935 |                     \"supported. If you tried to resample and interpolate on a \"\n936 |                     \"grouped data frame, please use:\\n\"\n937 |                     \"`df.groupby(...).apply(lambda x: x.resample(...).\"\n938 |                     \"interpolate(...), include_groups=False)`\"\n939 |                     \"\\ninstead, as resampling and interpolation has to be \"\n940 |                     \"performed for each group independently.\"\n941 |                 )\n942 | \n943 |             missing_data_points_index = obj.index.difference(final_index)\n944 |             if len(missing_data_points_index) > 0:\n945 |                 result = concat(\n946 |                     [result, obj.loc[missing_data_points_index]]\n947 |                 ).sort_index()\n948 | \n949 |         result_interpolated = result.interpolate(\n950 |             method=method,\n951 |             axis=axis,\n952 |             limit=limit,\n953 |             inplace=inplace,\n954 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n955 |             limit_area=limit_area,\n956 |             downcast=downcast,\n957 |             **kwargs,\n958 |         )\n959 | \n960 |         # No further steps if the original data has a PeriodIndex\n961 |         if is_period_index:\n962 |             return result_interpolated\n963 | \n964 |         # Make sure that original data points which do not align with the\n965 |         # resampled index are removed\n966 |         result_interpolated = result_interpolated.loc[final_index]\n967 | \n968 |         # Make sure frequency indexes are preserved\n969 |         result_interpolated.index = final_index\n970 |         return result_interpolated\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/arrays/base.py:ExtensionArray.interpolate`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 989 | def interpolate(\n 990 |         self,\n 991 |         *,\n 992 |         method: InterpolateOptions,\n 993 |         axis: int,\n 994 |         index: Index,\n 995 |         limit,\n 996 |         limit_direction,\n 997 |         limit_area,\n 998 |         copy: bool,\n 999 |         **kwargs,\n1000 |     ) -> Self:\n1001 |         \"\"\"\n1002 |         Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n1003 | \n1004 |         Parameters\n1005 |         ----------\n1006 |         method : str, default 'linear'\n1007 |             Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n1008 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally spaced.\n1009 |             This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n1010 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n1011 |             given length of interval.\n1012 |             * 'index', '