frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.rename_axis`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1103 | def rename_axis(\n1104 |         self,\n1105 |         mapper: IndexLabel | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n1106 |         *,\n1107 |         index=lib.no_default,\n1108 |         columns=lib.no_default,\n1109 |         axis: Axis = 0,\n1110 |         copy: bool | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n1111 |         inplace: bool = False,\n1112 |     ) -> Self | None:\n1113 |         \"\"\"\n1114 |         Set the name of the axis for the index or columns.\n1115 | \n1116 |         Parameters\n1117 |         ----------\n1118 |         mapper : scalar, list-like, optional\n1119 |             Value to set the axis name attribute.\n1120 | \n1121 |             Use either ``mapper`` and ``axis`` to\n1122 |             specify the axis to target with ``mapper``, or ``index``\n1123 |             and/or ``columns``.\n1124 |         index : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1125 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1126 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1127 |         columns : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1128 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1129 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1130 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns'}, default 0\n1131 |             The axis to rename.\n1132 |         copy : bool, default False\n1133 |             Also copy underlying data.\n1134 | \n1135 |             .. note::\n1136 |                 The `copy` keyword will change behavior in pandas 3.0.\n1137 |                 `Copy-on-Write\n1138 |                 <https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/user_guide/copy_on_write.html>`__\n1139 |                 will be enabled by default, which means that all methods with a\n1140 |                 `copy` keyword will use a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n1141 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n1142 |                 future version of pandas.\n1143 | \n1144 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n1145 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n1146 | \n1147 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n1148 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1149 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Series\n1150 |             or DataFrame.\n1151 | \n1152 |         Returns\n1153 |         -------\n1154 |         DataFrame, or None\n1155 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1156 | \n1157 |         See Also\n1158 |         --------\n1159 |         Series.rename : Alter Series index labels or name.\n1160 |         DataFrame.rename : Alter DataFrame index labels or name.\n1161 |         Index.rename : Set new names on index.\n1162 | \n1163 |         Notes\n1164 |         -----\n1165 |         ``DataFrame.rename_axis`` supports two calling conventions\n1166 | \n1167 |         * ``(index=index_mapper, columns=columns_mapper, ...)``\n1168 |         * ``(mapper, axis={'index', 'columns'}, ...)``\n1169 | \n1170 |         The first calling convention will only modify the names of\n1171 |         the index and/or the names of the Index object that is the columns.\n1172 |         In this case, the parameter ``copy`` is ignored.\n1173 | \n1174 |         The second calling convention will modify the names of the\n1175 |         corresponding index if mapper is a list or a scalar.\n1176 |         However, if mapper is dict-like or a function, it will use the\n1177 |         deprecated behavior of modifying the axis *labels*.\n1178 | \n1179 |         We *highly* recommend using keyword arguments to clarify your\n1180 |         intent.\n1181 | \n1182 |         Examples\n1183 |         --------\n1184 |         **DataFrame**\n1185 | \n1186 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1187 |         ...     {\"num_legs\": [4, 4, 2], \"num_arms\": [0, 0, 2]}, [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]\n1188 |         ... )\n1189 |         >>> df\n1190 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1191 |         dog            4         0\n1192 |         cat            4         0\n1193 |         monkey         2         2\n1194 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"animal\")\n1195 |         >>> df\n1196 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1197 |         animal\n1198 |         dog            4         0\n1199 |         cat            4         0\n1200 |         monkey         2         2\n1201 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"limbs\", axis=\"columns\")\n1202 |         >>> df\n1203 |         limbs   num_legs  num_arms\n1204 |         animal\n1205 |         dog            4         0\n1206 |         cat            4         0\n1207 |         monkey         2         2\n1208 | \n1209 |         **MultiIndex**\n1210 | \n1211 |         >>> df.index = pd.MultiIndex.from_product(\n1212 |         ...     [[\"mammal\"], [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]], names=[\"type\", \"name\"]\n1213 |         ... )\n1214 |         >>> df\n1215 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1216 |         type   name\n1217 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1218 |                cat            4         0\n1219 |                monkey         2         2\n1220 | \n1221 |         >>> df.rename_axis(index={\"type\": \"class\"})\n1222 |         limbs          num_legs  num_arms\n1223 |         class  name\n1224 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1225 |                cat            4         0\n1226 |                monkey         2         2\n1227 | \n1228 |         >>> df.rename_axis(columns=str.upper)\n1229 |         LIMBS          num_legs  num_arms\n1230 |         type   name\n1231 |         mammal dog            4         0\n1232 |                cat            4         0\n1233 |                monkey         2         2\n1234 |         \"\"\"\n1235 |         self._check_copy_deprecation(copy)\n1236 |         axes = {\"index\": index, \"columns\": columns}\n1237 | \n1238 |         if axis is not None:\n1239 |             axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n1240 | \n1241 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n1242 | \n1243 |         if mapper is not lib.no_default:\n1244 |             # Use v0.23 behavior if a scalar or list\n1245 |             non_mapper = is_scalar(mapper) or (\n1246 |                 is_list_like(mapper) and not is_dict_like(mapper)\n1247 |             )\n1248 |             if non_mapper:\n1249 |                 return self._set_axis_name(mapper, axis=axis, inplace=inplace)\n1250 |             else:\n1251 |                 raise ValueError(\"Use `.rename` to alter labels with a mapper.\")\n1252 |         else:\n1253 |             # Use new behavior.  Means that index and/or columns\n1254 |             # is specified\n1255 |             result = self if inplace else self.copy(deep=False)\n1256 | \n1257 |             for axis in range(self._AXIS_LEN):\n1258 |                 v = axes.get(self._get_axis_name(axis))\n1259 |                 if v is lib.no_default:\n1260 |                     continue\n1261 |                 non_mapper = is_scalar(v) or (is_list_like(v) and not is_dict_like(v))\n1262 |                 if non_mapper:\n1263 |                     newnames = v\n1264 |                 else:\n1265 |                     f = common.get_rename_function(v)\n1266 |                     curnames = self._get_axis(axis).names\n1267 |                     newnames = [f(name) for name in curnames]\n1268 |                 result._set_axis_name(newnames, axis=axis, inplace=True)\n1269 |             if not inplace:\n1270 |                 return result\n1271 |             return None\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n142 | def create_subplots(\n143 |     naxes: int,\n144 |     sharex: bool = False,\n145 |     sharey: bool = False,\n146 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n147 |     subplot_kw=None,\n148 |     ax=None,\n149 |     layout=None,\n150 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n151 |     **fig_kw,\n152 | ):\n153 |     \"\"\"\n154 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n155 | \n156 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n157 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n158 | \n159 |     Parameters\n160 |     ----------\n161 |     naxes : int\n162 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n163 |       nrows * ncols.\n164 | \n165 |     sharex : bool\n166 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n167 | \n168 |     sharey : bool\n169 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n170 | \n171 |     squeeze : bool\n172 | \n173 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n174 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n175 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n176 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n177 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n178 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n179 | \n180 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n181 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n182 | \n183 |     subplot_kw : dict\n184 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n185 |       subplots.\n186 | \n187 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n188 | \n189 |     layout : tuple\n190 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n191 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n192 | \n193 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n194 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n195 | \n196 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n197 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n198 |         automatically included here.\n199 | \n200 |     Returns\n201 |     -------\n202 |     fig, ax : tuple\n203 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n204 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n205 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n206 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n207 | \n208 |     Examples\n209 |     --------\n210 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n211 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n212 | \n213 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n214 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n215 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n216 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n217 | \n218 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n219 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n220 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n221 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n222 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n223 | \n224 |     # Four polar axes\n225 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n226 |     \"\"\"\n227 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n228 | \n229 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n230 |         subplot_kw = {}\n231 | \n232 |     if ax is None:\n233 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n234 |     else:\n235 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n236 |             if squeeze:\n237 |                 ax = np.fromiter(flatten_axes(ax), dtype=object)\n238 |             if layout is not None:\n239 |                 warnings.warn(\n240 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n241 |                     UserWarning,\n242 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n243 |                 )\n244 |             if sharex or sharey:\n245 |                 warnings.warn(\n246 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n247 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n248 |                     UserWarning,\n249 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n250 |                 )\n251 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n252 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 raise ValueError(\n256 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n257 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n258 |                 )\n259 | \n260 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n261 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n262 |         if naxes == 1:\n263 |             if squeeze:\n264 |                 return fig, ax\n265 |             else:\n266 |                 return fig, np.fromiter(flatten_axes(ax), dtype=object)\n267 |         else:\n268 |             warnings.warn(\n269 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n270 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n271 |                 UserWarning,\n272 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n273 |             )\n274 |             fig.clear()\n275 | \n276 |     nrows, n